{"id":"h092","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A cyclotron has an oscillator frequency of 11.4 Mc \\bullet s^-1 and a radius of 60 cm. What magnetic induction is required to accelerate protons of mass 1.67 x 10^-27 kg and charge 1.6 × 10^-1 9 C, and what is the final energy that they acquire? What error is made by assuming that the mass of the protons remains constant?\nIs the correct answer \"0.748Wb\bulletm^-2, 0.154 × 10^-11 J, 1.02%\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The stated answer to the question is correct (YES).","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"At resonance the cyclotron frequency equals the oscillator frequency, so B = 2πmf/q.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The required magnetic induction computes to about 0.748 T, matching the stated value.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The final proton energy is E = (qBr)²/(2m), about 1.54×10⁻¹² J (0.154×10⁻¹¹ J), matching the stated value.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The error from assuming constant mass is the fractional mass increase Δm/m ≈ E/(mc²), about 1.02%.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The 1.02% figure matches the stated answer and is the standard textbook reading of the question.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"If the error is instead measured as the kinetic energy discrepancy between classical and relativistic formulas, it would be about 0.6%, not 1%.","confidence":0.6,"lean":-1,"theta":0.9425,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The mass-increase interpretation is conventional and is what the question intends, so the 1.02% answer stands.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The B and E values follow directly from resonance and orbit relations and are decisive.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta"